  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: S&EDisc 5
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: 'Your research team is testing several chemical compounds that might
    one day become part of a new cancer treatment. A lab technician notices that
    some of the chemicals being used can irritate the skin or eyes. This makes her
    wonder if the Health & Safety Office requires a report or special approval every
    time a new compound is mixed or tested during this early discovery work.


    What
    is the role of the Health & Safety Office in the Discovery stage?'
  explanation: "The Health & Safety Office, often called Environmental Health & Safety
    (EHS), helps keep research labs safe and compliant with safety regulations. Although
    the EHS sets the safety rules for handling chemicals in the lab, such as wearing
    protective gear and storing materials properly, researchers don\u2019t need their
    approval for every individual experiment. By following the EHS safety rules,
    researchers can work safely as they investigate which compounds might eventually
    become useful cancer treatments."
  correctAnswer: 'D: They set overall safety rules and lab training requirements
    but do not approve every experiment at this stage.'
  incorrectAnswer1: 'A: They must review and approve every chemical lab experiment
    done in early discovery work.'
  incorrectAnswer2: 'B: They only get involved at this stage if the chemical compounds
    will be given to patient participants in a clinical trial.'
  incorrectAnswer3: 'C: They only manage building systems like temperature and airflow
    in the research labs.'
  questionRole: Safety & Ethics
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: Discovery
